---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Button from '../components/ui/Button.astro';
import { author } from '../data/author';

const mailto = `mailto:${author.email}?subject=Consulting%20inquiry`;
const calLink = 'https://cal.com/theo-hermann-aaa7tm';

const scopes = [
  {
    id: 'ros2-log-triage',
    title: 'ROS 2 fleet log triage',
    duration: '2–4 weeks',
    repoLine: 'Method public: github.com/thrmnn/ros2-localization-triage',
    summary:
      "A robot misbehaves at a customer site and someone has to comb the bag files and logs to find out why. I do that investigation: reconstruct the timeline, isolate the failure, and write up what happened with the evidence attached. Then I calibrate detectors to your fleet's failure classes — sensor dropout, transform jumps, covariance spikes, pose divergence — run against your recorded bags and logs. Calibration is not optional: the same frozen thresholds that stay quiet on one platform false-alarm from 9 to 1,880 times an hour on another.",
    deliverables: [
      'Incident report: timeline, root cause, supporting evidence from your bags and logs',
      "Detectors for your fleet's failure classes (sensor dropout, transform jumps, covariance spikes, pose divergence), run against your recorded bags and logs",
      'Thresholds calibrated to your fleet, with measured false-alarm rates',
      'Analysis scripts you can rerun on new recordings',
      'Analysis runs on your infrastructure or under NDA on mine; nothing is retained after handover.',
    ],
    evidence:
      'The method is public in <a href="https://github.com/thrmnn/ros2-localization-triage">ros2-localization-triage</a>: four localization-failure detectors run against 108 minutes of real recordings from five robot platforms — Cartographer backpack rigs, a Tiago, a MiR100 AGV, a PR2, and a handheld 3D rig, all third-party public benchmark data, not a live customer fleet — with a self-graded case log whose rubric was committed before the results existed, and wrong results published as wrong. Against failure labels written by the Cartographer dataset\'s own authors more than a decade before this tool existed, the gap detector found 16 of 16 labelled laser gaps and nothing else. A zero-false-positive prediction was written down and committed before a separate benchmark run — and held, across 1,013 seconds of matched-control data. One candidate dataset was rejected outright because its license didn\'t clear for commercial, paid work. Don\'t take any of this on faith — clone the repo and run python3 scripts/check_numbers.py; it recomputes every headline figure from committed data. The same frozen thresholds produce false-alarm rates from 9 per robot-hour on one platform to 1,880 on another — that gap is why per-fleet calibration, not detection, is the work a live engagement would do.',
  },
  {
    id: 'pipeline-audit',
    title: 'Perception pipeline audit',
    duration: '2 weeks',
    summary:
      'A focused review of your existing detection / tracking / pose stack. I read the code, trace the latency budget end-to-end, identify the blockers most likely to break in production, and hand back a written report with prioritized fixes.',
    deliverables: [
      'Architecture and data-flow walkthrough',
      'Per-module latency / accuracy breakdown',
      'Top-N prioritized findings with concrete fix recommendations',
      'Optional: 1-week implementation pass on the highest-leverage finding',
    ],
    evidence:
      'Roboat camera-first stack — <30 ms inference on Jetson Orin, deployed on canal vessels in Amsterdam. EVIO event-camera VIO at IRI Barcelona.',
  },
  {
    id: 'lidar-bringup',
    title: 'LiDAR / point-cloud pipeline bring-up',
    duration: '3–4 weeks',
    summary:
      'You have raw LiDAR but no production pipeline. I take you from raw scans to a usable processed product: calibration, registration, segmentation, ground extraction, and the downstream representation you actually need (mesh, occupancy grid, semantic point cloud).',
    deliverables: [
      'Sensor calibration + ground-truth alignment',
      'Registration / SLAM pass',
      'Semantic segmentation (ground, vegetation, structures)',
      'Documented Python / ROS pipeline, reproducible from raw',
    ],
    evidence:
      'MIT Senseable City Lab Rio — terrestrial LiDAR of Rio favelas fused with aerial multispectral and DTMs, feeding CFD airflow simulations. MSc thesis at MIT Senseable Amsterdam with AMS Institute: per-tree LAI from aerial LiDAR for the City of Amsterdam.',
  },
  {
    id: 'embodied-mvp',
    title: 'Robot-perception MVP',
    duration: '6–8 weeks',
    summary:
      'End-to-end detection + tracking + state-estimation prototype on real hardware. Targeted at teams who need to move from research code to a robot that runs reliably outside the lab. Scoped tightly: one platform, one task, hard latency budget.',
    deliverables: [
      'Hardware integration + sensor calibration on your platform',
      'Detection + tracking + (optional) ReID / pose primitives wired up',
      'TensorRT export and latency budget signed off',
      'Demo loop running on robot + handover documentation',
    ],
    evidence:
      'EPFL VITA Lab — full autonomy stack on Segway Loomo (SLAM + planning + obstacle avoidance + person-following), released open-source. Roboat — first engineering hire, perception lead through deployment.',
  },
  {
    id: 'agents-infra',
    title: 'AI-agent infrastructure',
    duration: '4–8 weeks',
    summary:
      'For teams shipping agentic products and hitting the wall every team hits — flaky tool calls, unobservable failures, no evals, no safe way to ship a prompt change. I build the harness around your agent so it behaves like a production system: typed tool layer, retrieval you can measure, traces you can actually read, and an eval suite that catches regressions before users do.',
    deliverables: [
      'Typed, versioned tool layer (testable in isolation)',
      'Retrieval pipeline with an offline eval harness',
      'Tracing + observability on every agent run',
      'Regression eval suite wired into CI',
    ],
    evidence:
      'Early open-source scaffold (<a href="https://github.com/thrmnn/agent-harness">agent-harness</a>): typed tool layer and eval-suite structure, started June 2026. The same engineering discipline demonstrated in <a href="https://github.com/thrmnn/ros2-localization-triage">ros2-localization-triage</a> — typed interfaces, tests, reproducible mechanical checks — is what an engagement in this scope would bring to your agent stack.',
  },
];
---

<PageLayout
  title="Consulting"
  description="Short, scoped engagements: ROS 2 fleet log triage, perception pipeline audits, LiDAR bring-up, robot-perception MVPs, AI-agent infrastructure."
>
  <section class="py-[var(--space-section)]">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-[var(--max-w-content)] px-6">
      <header class="mb-12">
        <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-text-primary mb-4">
          Short, scoped engagements in robot perception, autonomy, and data.
        </h1>
        <p class="text-text-secondary max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed">
          I take on focused engagements in perception, autonomy, LiDAR/3D, and robot operational
          data — logs, bags, telemetry, failures. The scopes below are the shapes I keep coming
          back to. Anything else, ask.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="space-y-10">
        {scopes.map((s) => (
          <article id={s.id} class="scope-card border border-border rounded-lg p-6 md:p-8 bg-bg-elevated">
            <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-3 mb-4">
              <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-text-primary">{s.title}</h2>
              <span class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent">
                {s.duration}
              </span>
            </div>

            {s.repoLine && (
              <p class="font-mono text-xs text-text-muted mb-4">{s.repoLine}</p>
            )}

            <p class="text-text-secondary leading-relaxed mb-6">{s.summary}</p>

            <div class="grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
              <div>
                <h3 class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-text-muted mb-3">
                  What you get
                </h3>
                <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-text-secondary">
                  {s.deliverables.map((d) => (
                    <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-accent mt-0.5">→</span><span>{d}</span></li>
                  ))}
                </ul>
              </div>
              <div>
                <h3 class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-text-muted mb-3">
                  Why me
                </h3>
                <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary leading-relaxed" set:html={s.evidence} />
              </div>
            </div>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>

      <section class="mt-16 border-t border-border pt-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-text-primary mb-4">How engagements work</h2>
        <ul class="space-y-3 text-text-secondary max-w-2xl">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-text-primary">Discovery call (free, 20 min)</strong> — we scope the
            problem and I tell you honestly whether it's a fit.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-text-primary">Written proposal</strong> — fixed scope, fixed
            timeline, fixed price. You see exactly what you're buying before you commit.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-text-primary">Weekly written updates</strong> — what shipped, what's
            next, what's at risk. No status meetings unless you want them.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-text-primary">Handover artifact</strong> — running code,
            documentation, and a written report. Engagements end clean.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-16 border-t border-border pt-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-text-primary mb-4">Start a conversation</h2>
        <p class="text-text-secondary mb-8 max-w-2xl">
          Booking is the fastest path. If your project doesn't fit the slot list, email and I'll
          read it.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-4">
          <Button href={calLink} external>Book a 20-min intro</Button>
          <Button href={mailto} variant="secondary">E-mail</Button>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </section>
</PageLayout>

<style>
  .scope-card {
    transition: border-color 0.2s ease, transform 0.2s ease;
  }
  .scope-card:hover {
    border-color: var(--color-border-hover);
  }
</style>
